title:: MIR2
summary:: Modern SuperCollider music-information retrieval and pitch evidence
categories:: Libraries>MIR

MIR2 is a native family of control-rate analyzers built from published signal-processing methods. Version 0.1 combines explicit spectral/onset descriptors with YIN and McLeod pitch analysis, periodicity, voicing evidence, confidence, and octave ambiguity.

section:: Spectral and onset analyzers

definitionlist::
## link::Classes/SpecCentroid2:: || Spectral center in Hz.
## link::Classes/SpecFlatness2:: || Geometric-to-arithmetic spectral ratio.
## link::Classes/SpecPcile2:: || Generalized rolloff/percentile frequency.
## link::Classes/MFCC2:: || Configurable Mel cepstral coefficients.
## link::Classes/FFTFlux2:: || Frame-to-frame spectral distance.
## link::Classes/Onsets2:: || Flux and SuperFlux-style onset trigger plus strength.
::

section:: Modern Pitch Analysis

definitionlist::
## link::Classes/PitchYIN:: || YIN F0, confidence, and voicing.
## link::Classes/PitchMPM:: || McLeod F0, clarity, and voicing.
## link::Classes/Pitch2:: || General facade with explicit octave ambiguity.
## link::Classes/Periodicity:: || Repeating-structure evidence without a required F0 decision.
## link::Classes/Voicing:: || Binary and continuous voiced/harmonic evidence.
::

Pitch confidence, periodicity, and voicing are deliberately different outputs. A signal can be strongly periodic while offering several plausible fundamental frequencies; pitch-specific classes therefore should not be used as interchangeable probability meters.

section:: Frame policy

FFT analyzers update only when their FFT chain produces a frame. Their code::fftSize:: argument must match the buffer used by code::FFT::. Pitch analyzers accept audio-rate input, fill a trailing causal frame, and update once per code::hop * frameSize:: input samples. Their approximate first-result latency is code::frameSize / sampleRate:: seconds, with no future lookahead.

Silence returns zeros rather than NaN, Inf, or a stale pitch. code::Pitch2:: holds the last valid frequency only when code::hold: 1:: is explicitly requested.

section:: Runnable overview

code::
(
Routine({
    var buffer;
    buffer = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.child);
    s.sync;
    SynthDef(\mir2Guide, {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, buffer, BufRateScale.kr(buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, hop: 0.5);
        var pitch = Pitch2.kr(source, 40, 2000, 2048, 0.25, \mpm);
        var periodicity = Periodicity.kr(source, 40, 2000, 2048);
        var centroid = SpecCentroid2.kr(chain, fftSize: 1024);
        SendReply.kr(Impulse.kr(5), '/mir2', [pitch, periodicity, centroid].flat);
        Out.ar(0, (source * 0.15) ! 2)
    }).play;
}).play(AppClock);
)
::

section:: Project boundary

MIR2 is complementary to FluCoMa. Use FluCoMa for corpus, decomposition, dimensionality-reduction, and data workflows; use MIR2 for its modern legacy successors, explicit pitch evidence, and later tonal/rhythm descriptors. Version 0.1 does not install placeholders for probabilistic candidates, SWIPE', multipitch, neural inference, or the later MIR catalogue.

section:: References

PitchYIN follows de Cheveigné and Kawahara (2002). PitchMPM follows McLeod and Wyvill (2005). Onsets2's maximum-filtered method follows the architectural idea in Böck and Widmer (2013). See the installed ORIGINS.md for complete provenance and scope.
